Let's analyze the provided code and the comment to derive the appropriate assert statement.

The method `pollFirst()` is supposed to retrieve and remove the first element of the deque if it is non-empty. The comment states:

```java
// @@@ If the collection was nonempty, polling decreases its size by one.
```

This means that if the deque had elements before the `pollFirst()` call, then after the call, the size of the deque should be one less than it was before the call.

To express this in an assert statement, we need to compare the size of the deque before and after the `pollFirst()` call. We can use the `\old()` syntax to refer to the size of the deque before the method call.

Here's the assert statement that captures this requirement:

<code>assert !\old(this.isEmpty()) => \old(this.size()) == this.size() + 1;</code>

This statement ensures that if the deque was non-empty before the `pollFirst()` call (`!\old(this.isEmpty())`), then the size of the deque after the call (`this.size()`) is one less than it was before the call (`\old(this.size()) == this.size() + 1`).
